replace(r'\title{Evaluating Learned Skeleton Features in Neurological Gait Videos}',r'\title{Learning Skeleton Features for Neurological Gait Video Classification}')
section(r'\begin{abstract}',r'\section{Introduction}',r'''\begin{abstract}
Learning from video-derived body landmarks can complement handcrafted gait descriptions. We evaluate a compact skeleton joint-embedding predictive architecture (S-JEPA) on 88 usable clips from 41 source recordings labeled Normal, multiple sclerosis, or Parkinson's disease. The collection labels were not clinically verified. Each encoder is trained without condition labels on 45--58 clips from 24 sources by predicting hidden skeleton features. A linear classifier then uses the resulting features to predict labels for held-out recordings. Source recordings remain separate during training, model selection, and testing. Macro-F1, which averages F1 across the three labels, is 0.457 for S-JEPA, 0.452 for mean pose, and 0.411 for the handcrafted joint-angle Random Forest (RF) when every recording receives equal total weight. We separately report the RF evaluation after correcting a duplicated ankle-range feature. On this small collection, the trained S-JEPA features supported label prediction and achieved a modest observed improvement over the evaluated handcrafted RF under equal-source scoring. These findings provide a practical starting point for predictive skeleton learning when gait recordings are limited.
\end{abstract}''')
replace('Validation studies of video-derived gait measures~\\cite{stenum2021,stenum2024} and work on Parkinson\'s disease (PD)~\\cite{shin2021} motivate this direction without validating every subsequent classifier trained on video.', 'Validation studies of video-derived gait measures~\\cite{stenum2021,stenum2024} and work on Parkinson\'s disease (PD)~\\cite{shin2021} motivate the use of estimated body landmarks in movement research. Learning useful descriptions from those landmarks is a related question, particularly when only a small collection of labeled videos is available.')
replace('We investigate whether a model that learns numerical summaries of skeleton sequences provides a consistent classification advantage over simple pose averages in a small collection labeled Normal, MS, and PD.', 'We investigate whether a compact model trained on skeleton sequences produces features useful for classifying a small collection labeled Normal, MS, and PD. We compare those features with a handcrafted joint-angle Random Forest and simple pose summaries.')
replace('The empirical contribution is a bounded comparison: the learned model\'s small overall lead over mean pose reverses when clips, rather than source recordings, receive equal weight. The learned model leads in only one of the five test groups. We compare both methods and a visibility-only summary on the same source partitions, so readers can assess the extent and consistency of the differences. This makes the study relevant to behavioral phenotyping from markerless pose: it examines what a small video collection can support before the output is interpreted as a clinical measure. It contains no neural recordings or direct neural decoding experiment.', 'The study demonstrates a working application of predictive skeleton learning with limited recordings: each model is trained on 24 sources, and its fixed features support label prediction on other source recordings. S-JEPA gives the highest observed score when every recording receives equal weight, ahead of the tested joint-angle RF and close to mean pose. This supports the feasibility of using trained skeleton features in this setting, while the strong mean-pose result gives a useful reference for assessing what more complex sequence models add. The documented processing and saved predictions also allow the reported comparison to be checked directly. The contribution concerns behavioral analysis from markerless pose; the study contains no neural recordings or direct neural decoding experiment.')
replace('Small-sample model evaluation can have large uncertainty, and using the same data for model selection and evaluation can bias the reported error~\\cite{varoquaux2018,varma2006}. We therefore separate training, validation, and outer-test sources. This controls source overlap within the specified evaluation procedure, although it cannot account for every decision made during development on this collection.', 'Reliable comparison requires recordings used to choose a model to remain separate from those used to assess it~\\cite{varoquaux2018,varma2006}. We reserve distinct source groups for training, validation, and testing, and evaluate all methods on the same test groups. This separation addresses repeated clips from a recording while leaving the broader question of transfer to new participants for a verified clinical collection.')
section(r'\subsection{Scoring}',r'\section{Results}',r'''\subsection{Comparing predictions}
F1 balances how often a predicted label is correct with how many examples of that label are found. We calculate it separately for Normal, MS, and PD, then average the three values to obtain macro-F1. Higher values indicate better classification, with one representing perfect predictions. The score is different from the fraction of correctly classified clips.

Our main comparison gives every source recording equal total weight, preventing a recording with many clips from dominating the result. Each clip from a source with $n_s$ clips receives weight $1/n_s$; for example, the 13 clips from one MS source each receive weight $1/13$. We combine all held-out predictions using these weights before calculating macro-F1. We also give every clip equal weight in a second comparison. The final column of Table~\ref{tab:results} reports the mean score across the five test groups and their standard deviation (SD), which describes how much those scores vary. Averaging the group scores can give a different value from scoring all predictions together.

We additionally check how the results depend on which recordings are included by resampling whole sources and recalculating scores 10,000 times. All clips from a sampled source stay together, each label retains its source count, and all methods use the same sampled sources. The middle 95\% of these recalculated differences is reported once in Results. This check uses saved predictions rather than retraining the models. Exact formulas, seed and resampling details are provided in \texttt{evidence/v9-methods.md}, together with the limits of this additional analysis.''')
replace('Table~\\ref{tab:results} reports the primary notebook-defined experiment, with scores checked against its 88 saved outer-test rows. S-JEPA reaches source-weighted macro-F1 0.457 and mean pose reaches 0.452, a difference of 0.0047. Under clip weighting their ordering reverses: mean pose reaches 0.410 and S-JEPA 0.397. Mean pose correctly classifies 37 clips and S-JEPA 36. One MS source contributes 13 clips, making the choice of weighting consequential.', 'Table~\\ref{tab:results} reports the primary notebook-defined experiment, verified against all 88 saved test predictions. S-JEPA reaches macro-F1 0.457 when recordings receive equal weight, compared with 0.411 for the handcrafted joint-angle RF---an observed improvement of 0.0454. Its features also give higher scores than the tested visibility-only and majority-label baselines. A linear classifier can therefore use the trained representation to predict collection labels on held-out source recordings. This is a useful result for a compact model trained on only 45--58 clips per split.')
replace('The conditional interval for S-JEPA macro-F1 is $[0.329,0.579]$. Its paired difference from mean pose is $0.0047$ with interval $[-0.160,0.174]$; against the notebook RF it is $0.0454$ with interval $[-0.133,0.220]$. Both span zero. The data and this conditional procedure leave the directions uncertain without showing that the methods are equivalent.', 'Mean pose provides a strong comparison, reaching 0.452 when recordings receive equal weight. The S-JEPA lead over it is 0.0047. When clips receive equal weight, mean pose reaches 0.410, while S-JEPA and the notebook RF both round to 0.397; RF is slightly higher before rounding. Mean pose and RF each correctly classify 37 clips, compared with 36 for S-JEPA. The advantage over RF thus concerns the equal-recording score, rather than the number of correctly classified clips. This distinction matters because one MS recording contributes 13 clips.')
replace('Mean pose exceeds S-JEPA in four of five outer folds (Figure~\\ref{fig:results}a). The pooled difference consequently does not describe a consistent foldwise improvement. The visibility-only score is lower than both coordinate-based representations, but this comparison cannot quantify how much recording information remains in their features. Mean pose retains average body configuration and can reflect time spent in different positions, even though it discards their order. Its competitiveness therefore does not imply that all movement-related information has been removed. S-JEPA fold scores range from 0.217 to 0.780. Its raw correct counts are 10/24 Normal, 8/29 MS, and 18/35 PD clips; source-weighted recalls are 0.396, 0.429, and 0.569 (Figure~\\ref{fig:results}b). The average score therefore coexists with substantial errors in all three labels.', 'Performance varies across the test groups (Figure~\\ref{fig:results}a). S-JEPA scores range from 0.217 to 0.780, and mean pose is higher in four of the five groups. Mean pose retains average body configuration and can reflect time spent in different positions even though it discards their order, which helps explain why it is an informative control for a sequence model. S-JEPA correctly classifies 10/24 Normal, 8/29 MS, and 18/35 PD clips. When recordings receive equal weight, the corresponding fractions of each label recovered are 0.396, 0.429, and 0.569 (Figure~\\ref{fig:results}b). These class-level results identify substantial room to improve all three predictions.\n\nResampling the saved predictions gives a middle-95\\% range of $[-0.133,0.220]$ for the S-JEPA minus RF difference and $[-0.160,0.174]$ for S-JEPA minus mean pose. These ranges show that the observed score advantages can change with the sampled recordings. They describe variation within this collection while holding the trained models fixed; they do not establish how either method will perform in a new clinical population. The complete calculation, including the S-JEPA score range $[0.329,0.579]$, is retained in the companion methods record.')
replace('The initial stage is selected in folds zero through two, and continuation in folds three and four. The final training batch has lower prediction error after continuation in every fold, yet the validation classification score improves in only two. Lower error on that sampled batch therefore did not reliably identify the stage selected for classification. Because the endpoint batches differ, these values do not measure average prediction error on a common evaluation set.', 'Validation selects the initial training stage in test groups zero through two and the continued stage in groups three and four. The choice follows classification performance on separate validation recordings.')
replace('The corrected RF yields source-weighted macro-F1 0.325 and clip-weighted macro-F1 0.321, compared with 0.411 and 0.397 in the notebook-reported implementation. S-JEPA minus corrected RF is $0.1314$ with conditional interval $[-0.046,0.304]$. A correction can lower performance; the direction alone says nothing about which implementation faithfully computes the intended feature. This verification does not establish superiority over a well-tuned classical gait system. Both saved executions use the same data, split, and training seed, and their S-JEPA hard predictions coincide despite different embedding values. They are not independent-seed replications.', 'The corrected RF gives macro-F1 0.325 when recordings receive equal weight and 0.321 when clips receive equal weight, compared with 0.411 and 0.397 for the notebook-reported version. S-JEPA remains higher under equal-recording scoring, with a difference of 0.1314; its resampling range is $[-0.046,0.304]$. The feature correction lowers this RF score, illustrating why feature correctness must be checked independently of the resulting accuracy. Both executions use the same data, split, and training seed, and their S-JEPA predicted labels coincide despite different feature vectors. This is a verification of the baseline implementation, rather than an independent repetition with new seeds or recordings. It compares the specific RF settings used here and does not cover more extensively tuned classical gait systems.')
section(r'\section{Discussion and limitations}',r'{\small'+'\n'+r'\bibliographystyle',r'''\section{Discussion and limitations}
The study supports the practical use of trained skeleton features for predicting labels in a small gait-video collection. The encoder receives no condition labels during feature training, yet its fixed numerical descriptions can be used by a linear classifier on held-out recordings. Under equal-recording scoring, this combination exceeds the tested handcrafted joint-angle RF, including its separately verified feature correction. The result offers a concrete reason to investigate predictive skeleton features alongside established gait summaries when recordings are limited.

The observed benefit applies to these complete procedures. S-JEPA and the RF differ in feature construction and classifier, and only S-JEPA selects between two training stages. Their score difference therefore cannot identify which component accounts for the gain. Here, useful features means information that supports prediction of the supplied collection labels; their relationship to specific movement impairments remains to be established. Comparing the trained encoder with the same network at initialization would measure what training adds. Testing a matched change in frame order would help determine the contribution of movement sequence. Neither experiment was performed here. Mean pose's competitive results make it a valuable reference in those follow-up comparisons.

A known implementation issue concerns reflection during training. Reflection exchanges left and right landmark slots in the context input, while the mask and unreflected target retain their original indices. A physical joint selected as a target can consequently remain visible in the opposite-side context slot. This mismatch occurs within training examples; its effect on classification was not measured. A comparison with corrected correspondence or with reflection disabled would determine its importance. The anatomical mask choice likewise remains a design hypothesis without a separate performance comparison.

The collection is small and heterogeneous, and the training uses one random seed. Different source identifiers may still contain the same participant, and the supplied labels are not verified diagnoses. Viewpoint, pose-estimation errors, interpolation and repeated-frame padding can all influence the input. Body normalization removes absolute pelvis movement and apparent torso size, so the learned descriptions are not calibrated measurements of walking speed or stride length. The visibility-only comparison cannot rule out recording-related information in the other representations.

The available records do not establish participant consent for secondary health inference, a complete assessment of source-media rights, or an ethics-review determination. These require documentation before a release or clinical follow-up. The present predictions do not support individual clinical decisions. A larger study with verified identities and diagnoses could assess transfer to new participants and recording sites, and test whether predicted confidence agrees with observed accuracy.

\section{Conclusion}
A compact S-JEPA pipeline trained on a small collection of gait videos produced features that supported prediction of the collection labels on held-out source recordings. It achieved the highest observed macro-F1 when each recording received equal weight: 0.457, compared with 0.411 for the tested handcrafted joint-angle RF and 0.452 for mean pose. This modest observed improvement over the RF provides an encouraging starting point for predictive skeleton learning with limited video data. The strong mean-pose comparison and documented implementation checks provide a clear basis for the next experiments: establish what training and frame order add, then assess the resulting features in an independent, clinically verified collection.''')
